      "writtenOn": "2022-09-28T18:31:08Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "The change breaks the singleton pattern: this will now cache the very first effect, and (incorrectly) reuse the same result for all other inputs.\n\nI recall there was some talk about whether caching at this level is needed at all, as SkSL may handle it internally.  Brian, do you think it\u0027s still worth caching the effect, or should we just build on the fly?  I can run some local tests to see the perf impact.",
      "range": {
        "startLine": 221,
        "startChar": 4,
        "endLine": 221,
        "endChar": 16
      },
      "revId": "ed9c42375b81eb56dccfe066de8a58b1fc8fa14f",
      "serverId": "5086850b-e599-37d2-8ec8-ff16aeccbf02"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": true,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "c0a8f9ae_935d5757",
        "filename": "modules/skottie/src/effects/FractalNoiseEffect.cpp",
        "patchSetId": 3
      },
      "lineNbr": 221,
      "author": {
        "id": 5631
      },
      "writtenOn": "2022-09-28T18:38:08Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "We won\u0027t be caching this internally, and I\u0027d guess that you\u0027re seeing performance wins from this. I\u0027d suggest running the test locally, though. John and I were also talking about using something like the internal sksl modules for the skottie code, which would lend itself to a different pattern here. That\u0027s a more radical change, though (and we don\u0027t have a way to supply \"external\" modules, any module code today is baked into the sksl compiler).",
      "parentUuid": "a9743481_3c5f27ec",
      "range": {
        "startLine": 221,
        "startChar": 4,
        "endLine": 221,
        "endChar": 16
      },
      "revId": "ed9c42375b81eb56dccfe066de8a58b1fc8fa14f",
      "serverId": "5086850b-e599-37d2-8ec8-ff16aeccbf02"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": true,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "3c2aebbe_1ebda2a6",
        "filename": "modules/skottie/src/effects/FractalNoiseEffect.cpp",
        "patchSetId": 3
      },
      "lineNbr": 221,
      "author": {
        "id": 5642
      },
      "writtenOn": "2022-09-28T19:10:12Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "Alternatively, we can keep the cache but refactor to get rid of templates.  How about something along these lines:\n\n    static SkRuntimeEffect* gEffectCache[kMaxLoops]\n                                        [std::size(gFilters)]\n                                        [std::size(gFractals)];\n\n    auto\u0026 effect \u003d gEffectCache[loops]\n                               [static_cast\u003csize_t\u003e(filter)]\n                               [static_cast\u003csize_t\u003e(fractal)];\n\n    if (!effect) {\n        effect \u003d make_noise_effect(loops,\n                                   gFilters[static_cast\u003csize_t\u003e(filter)],\n                                   gFractals[static_cast\u003csize_t\u003e(fractal)])\n                 .release();\n    }\n\n    SkASSERT(effect);\n    return sk_ref_sp(effect);\n    \nI guess we lose thread safety, but this is only being called during revalidation (Animation::seek), which is not thread safe to begin with.\n\n(also we should tweak to only allocate 6 loop indices, since we never use all 20)",
      "parentUuid": "c0a8f9ae_935d5757",
      "range": {
        "startLine": 221,
        "startChar": 4,
        "endLine": 221,
        "endChar": 16
      },
      "revId": "ed9c42375b81eb56dccfe066de8a58b1fc8fa14f",
      "serverId": "5086850b-e599-37d2-8ec8-ff16aeccbf02"
    },
    {
